  > The cutout may be too shallow to contain the complete near-CMB
  > Sdiff–ULVZ interaction. Stacey boundaries can introduce both
  > spurious reflections, especially for grazing components, and loss
  > of physical exterior-mediated feedback when scattered waves would
  > leave and later re-enter the retained domain.

  The clean diagnostic is a radial-boundary convergence test: keep the
  ULVZ and lateral mesh identical, progressively move only the top and
  bottom boundaries outward. If the waveforms converge as radial
  padding increases—even with the same Stacey formulation—then
  mechanism (2), together with reduced grazing-boundary interaction, is
  implicated. Comparing Stacey with a better absorber at fixed
  dimensions helps separate the two mechanisms, but PML alone still
  cannot reproduce genuine exterior return paths; that requires
  enlarging the domain or coupling it to an exterior solver.
